<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern is a state in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on the Baltic coast, with Schwerin as its capital and Rostock as its largest city. It unites the historic lands of Mecklenburg with the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> left in Germany after 1945, including the island of Rügen. Created in 1945, broken up into districts by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a> in 1952 and re-established in 1990, it is the most thinly populated German state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern.jpg" alt="Mecklenburg-Vorpommern" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mecklenburg-Western_Pomerania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
